Recursion –

✅ Definition
Recursion is a technique where a function calls itself to solve a smaller version of the same problem.
It continues until a Base Case is reached.

✅ Two Important Parts
Base Case → Stops the recursion.
Recursive Case → Calls the function again with a smaller input.

✅ Syntax
def fun(n):
    if n == 0:      # Base Case
        return
    fun(n-1)        # Recursive Call

✅ How It Works
Function calls itself.
Each call is stored in the Call Stack.
When the base case is reached, the functions return one by one (Stack Unwinding).

✅ Example (Factorial)
def fact(n):
    if n == 0:
        return 1
    return n * fact(n-1)
Flow: fact(3) → fact(2) → fact(1) → fact(0) → returns 1 → 2 → 6

✅ Advantages
Short and clean code.
Best for Trees, Graphs, Backtracking, and Divide & Conquer problems.

✅ Disadvantages
Uses extra memory (Call Stack).
Slower than loops in some cases.
Missing a base case causes RecursionError (Stack Overflow).

✅ Common Applications
Factorial
Fibonacci
Binary Search
Tree Traversal (DFS)
Merge Sort
Quick Sort
Backtracking (N-Queens, Sudoku)

🎯 Points to Remember
Recursion = Function calls itself.
Base Case = Stops recursion.
Call Stack = Stores function calls.
No Base Case = Infinite recursion → RecursionError.
Best for recursive data structures (Trees/Graphs).
